# Hook and test scripts are executed by bash — CRLF breaks the shebang and
# `\r` leaks into strings, so pin them to LF regardless of core.autocrlf.
hooks/t4-session-start   text eol=lf
hooks/t4-prompt-reminder text eol=lf
hooks/t4-gate            text eol=lf
hooks/run-hook.cmd       text eol=lf
tests/**/*.sh            text eol=lf

# using-t4 is INJECTED verbatim into every session and measured against a byte
# budget (tests/hooks/test-dispatcher-content.sh). Unpinned, a Windows checkout
# gives it CRLF, and JSON-escaping the injection spends four bytes per line
# ending instead of two — silently costing ~2 bytes per line of budget that the
# guard, run on an LF working copy, never sees.
skills/t4/using-t4/SKILL.md  text eol=lf
skills/t4/t4-project-bootstrap/references/hooks/t4-session-start   text eol=lf
skills/t4/t4-project-bootstrap/references/hooks/t4-prompt-reminder text eol=lf
skills/t4/t4-project-bootstrap/references/hooks/t4-gate            text eol=lf
skills/t4/t4-project-bootstrap/references/hooks/run-hook.cmd       text eol=lf

# Guard scripts are executed by git as POSIX sh — CRLF breaks the shebang.
skills/t4/t4-project-bootstrap/references/guards/*  text eol=lf

# The INSTALLED copies in this repo, which tests/hooks/test-bootstrap-sync.sh
# compares byte-for-byte against the canonical sources pinned above. Unpinned,
# a Windows checkout gives them CRLF while the sources stay LF, and the sync
# test reports DRIFTED on files whose contents are identical — the same failure
# mode as the using-t4 budget note above, and the second time it has been hit.
# The comparison is byte-exact, so BOTH sides have to be pinned or neither is.
.claude/hooks/*  text eol=lf
.githooks/*      text eol=lf

# THE PIN HAS TO BE A GLOB ON BOTH SIDES (#260). The four `hooks/` lines above were
# written when that directory held four files; it now holds two dozen, and every hook
# added since was unpinned HERE while its installed copy was pinned by the
# `.claude/hooks/*` glob below. A Windows checkout then gives the two sides opposite
# endings and the byte-exact sync tests report DRIFTED on identical content — the third
# time this failure mode has been hit, and the first time the fix is not another list.
# `tests/hooks/test-line-ending-pins.sh` asserts the class, so a hook added tomorrow is
# covered on the day it lands.
hooks/*                                            text eol=lf
skills/t4/t4-project-bootstrap/references/hooks/*  text eol=lf

# A skill's VERSION is the sha256 of its SKILL.md BYTES (tests/hooks/test-skill-version.sh:
# "the version is the sha of the file itself"). Unpinned, the same commit hashes
# differently per checkout, so docs/research/rule-traces.md publishes one machine's
# answer and every other machine reports a mismatch it cannot fix without changing the
# file. using-t4's SKILL.md was already pinned above, for the adjacent byte-budget reason.
skills/**/SKILL.md  text eol=lf
